1785605956929322	sigil-dev	itc:v3:AWLOeSZkkyTnmRAAAAAAQd3q2q1d7QAAAAExCA		item.create	bn-3v9e	{"description":"Field report from sigil (maw 1.0.0-pre.12), 2026-07-31. Feeds the v1.0 dogfood window (bn-25db AC#3).\n\n# Workload\n\nA 4-workspace merge train in ~/src/sigil, run by a lead agent with three concurrent subagent workers. Three workspaces created from main in parallel (bn-2btu, bn-3hfd, bn-lz2d), each committed independently green under `just check`, then merged sequentially into default by the lead. Two pairs overlapped by construction: bn-3hfd and bn-lz2d both edited src/deploy/docker.rs; bn-2btu and bn-lz2d both edited CHANGELOG.md and src/cli/mod.rs. `maw ws overlap` predicted both pairs exactly, including the specific paths, before any merge ran.\n\nAll four merges landed. Final trunk is green: 2680 tests, clippy pedantic+nursery clean. But one finding below would have put a non-compiling tree on main if the lead had trusted maw's own success message instead of re-reading the file.\n\n# Finding 1 (severe) — `maw ws resolve --keep <ws-name>` silently reverted a hand-resolved line, then reported the workspace ready to merge\n\nThis is the one worth acting on. It is a correctness bug with a misleading success message, and the two together are what make it dangerous.\n\n## What happened\n\nAfter merging bn-2btu and bn-3hfd, sibling bn-lz2d had conflicts in CHANGELOG.md and src/deploy/docker.rs. Both were pure additive conflicts, so they were hand-resolved in the workspace, verified with a full `just check` (exit 0), and committed.\n\n`maw ws merge` then still reported one unresolved structured conflict in src/deploy/docker.rs. Neither `--keep epoch` nor `--keep bn-lz2d` is semantically \"the merged content I already produced\", but the workspace file *was* the correct merge — verified by grep before running anything: 12 markers unique to bn-3hfd's side (run_to_completion, drain_pipe, COMMAND_POLL_INTERVAL) and 4 unique to bn-lz2d's side (DeployStageTag, classify_deploy_stage), against 12 for the same bn-3hfd markers on the epoch. So `--keep bn-lz2d` looked correct: keep the side that already contains both.\n\nmaw then reported:\n\n```\n  resolved: src/deploy/docker.rs (3-way merge: ws intent on top of epoch, ws wins on overlap)\n\nAll structured conflicts resolved and committed (0eaf7405bb51). Workspace is ready for merge.\n```\n\nIt was not ready for merge. `--keep <ws-name>` is not \"take this side's file\" — it re-runs a 3-way merge, and on the one line where the two sides overlapped it took the workspace's version and discarded the epoch's:\n\n```rust\n// before resolve (hand-resolved, compiled, just check green):\nuse std::time::{Duration, Instant};\n\n// after resolve (silently reverted):\nuse std::time::Duration;\n```\n\n`Instant` was still referenced at four sites in that same file (lines 223, 243, 567, 601 — `Instant::now()` in the timeout deadline logic and two test helpers). The tree did not compile. maw called it resolved, committed it, and declared the workspace mergeable.\n\n## Why this is worse than it looks\n\nThe overlap was an import line — the highest-traffic, lowest-attention line in any Rust file, and precisely the kind of line a human or agent skims past. \"ws wins on overlap\" is a defensible default in the abstract, but applying it to a line the user had *already manually resolved* discards a decision that was more informed than either input side. The hand-resolved line was the union; maw replaced it with one operand.\n\n## Reproducer\n\n1. Two workspaces off the same base, both editing the same import line in one file (A adds `Instant`, B adds an unrelated `use crate::...`).\n2. Merge A into default.\n3. B now conflicts. Hand-resolve B's copy to the union, `git add && git commit` in the workspace.\n4. `maw ws merge B` reports a remaining structured conflict.\n5. `maw ws resolve B --keep B` → reports resolved and ready; the committed file has lost A's import; `cargo build` fails.\n\n## Impact\n\n~4 extra turns to detect and repair, and it required distrusting an affirmative success message. It did not reach main only because the lead re-read the resolved file rather than believing \"ready for merge\".\n\nMitigation that exists but should not be load-bearing: sigil configures a post-merge VALIDATE stage (`.maw/manifold/config.toml`, `cargo check --all-targets`, `on_failure = block-quarantine`, from maw bn-3ofy), which very likely would have caught this at merge time. Two reasons that is not a fix:\n1. `.maw/` is gitignored, so `[merge.validation]` is per-clone manifold state that does not propagate through push or merges. Any clone without it takes the broken merge.\n2. A resolve that silently discards the user's own edit is wrong independently of whether a downstream gate notices.\n\n## Suggested fixes\n\n- `--keep <side>` should mean take that side's blob verbatim. If a 3-way merge is wanted, that is a different flag (`--merge`, or make it opt-in).\n- Whatever the semantics, when resolve's output differs from every input side, say so and show the diff of what it changed. Silently producing a fourth version of the file is the core defect.\n- Do not print \"ready for merge\" from a command that cannot know the tree builds. \"Resolved and committed <sha> — verify the result builds before merging\" is honest and costs nothing.\n\nRelated: bn-2cc1 (closed) fixed a different failure behind the same sentence — resolve said \"ready for merge\" when the user still had to `git commit`. This is a second, worse instance of that message overpromising. The sentence itself may be the thing to retire.\n\n# Finding 2 (moderate) — `maw ws resolve` writes a header that maw's own merge gate rejects\n\nResolving CHANGELOG.md prepended four lines to the file:\n\n```\n# structured conflict at CHANGELOG.md\n# base blob: 2802108d826bf6f1687d87c219a06fb719ebe953\n# side epoch blob: d4ba33caa50500b9aa0890d928990481246aeb13\n# side bn-lz2d blob: db0e667b2375b17bf9f6f54f37b5504ea13cad4b\n```\n\nThe subsequent merge then refused:\n\n```\nError: Workspace 'bn-lz2d' has 1 path(s) that appear manually resolved but still begin with the maw conflict header:\n  - CHANGELOG.md\n  Delete the leading '#' header lines (...), commit, and re-run the merge.\n  This check cannot be bypassed by --force because merging a blob that still carries the maw conflict header would corrupt the target branch.\n```\n\nThe guard is good — see the positives section. The problem is that maw wrote the header and then blocked on it. One maw command produces a state another maw command declares corrupting, and the user is asked to hand-delete it in between. Either resolve should strip its own header when it writes the resolution, or the merge gate should strip it, but a tool should not reject its own output.\n\nAlso note the header is `#`-prefixed, which is a comment in TOML/YAML/shell/Python but *content* in Markdown — in CHANGELOG.md those four lines rendered as headings. For a language where `#` is not a comment, this silently corrupts the file rather than being inert.\n\n# Finding 3 (moderate) — conflict-reporting surfaces disagree; possible regression of bn-8zqz\n\n`maw ws conflicts bn-lz2d`:\n\n```\nWARNING: No merge conflicts detected, BUT the following workspace(s) have unresolved conflict markers committed into HEAD (probably from a prior `maw ws sync`):\n  bn-lz2d:\n    - CHANGELOG.md\n    - src/deploy/docker.rs\n```\n\nBoth were hand-resolved and committed. `maw ws merge bn-lz2d` then reported a conflict that `conflicts` had just said did not exist:\n\n```\nError: Workspace 'bn-lz2d' has 1 unresolved conflict(s):\n  - src/deploy/docker.rs\n```\n\nSo `conflicts` says \"no merge conflicts detected\" in the same breath as listing two conflicted files, and its assessment does not survive contact with `merge`. bn-8zqz (\"Conflict state inconsistent across ws conflicts / merge --check / resolve --list; stale metadata needs manual sync\") is closed and describes this class exactly. Worth checking whether this is a regression or a surface bn-8zqz did not cover.\n\nPractical consequence for agents: there is no single command whose answer about conflict state can be trusted, so the safe pattern is to attempt the merge and read its error — which makes `conflicts` and `--check` close to useless as pre-flight.\n\n# Finding 4 (minor) — auto-sync commits conflict markers into a workspace's HEAD\n\nMerging bn-2btu and bn-3hfd each ended with:\n\n```\nNOTE: 1 sibling workspace(s) now have conflicts: bn-lz2d\n```\n\nand bn-lz2d's HEAD had acquired committed diff3 markers (`<<<<<<< epoch (current)` / `||||||| base` / `=======` / `>>>>>>> bn-lz2d (workspace changes)`). Committing markers means the workspace HEAD no longer builds, so any agent still working there gets confusing failures unrelated to its own edits, and `git stash`/`git diff` workflows in that workspace now operate on a poisoned tree. Leaving the conflict in the working tree unstaged, or parking it on a side ref, would be less destructive than committing it.\n\nThe mitigating half is good: the notification is prompt and names the affected sibling.\n\n# What worked well (please do not regress these)\n\n- **`maw ws overlap A B` is excellent.** It predicted both collision pairs and the exact paths before any merge ran, and \"Risk: high\" was accurate both times. This is what let the work be scheduled correctly — bn-lz2d was deliberately merged last because overlap said it would collide with the other two. Best command in the tool for multi-agent work.\n- **The conflict-header guard refusing `--force`.** Explicitly stating that the check cannot be bypassed *and why* (\"would corrupt the target branch\") is exactly right. Guards that explain their own irreducibility are rare and worth keeping.\n- **`maw epoch sync`'s diagnostic.** The first merge failed with \"Target branch 'main' has diverged from the current epoch / Direct commits were made to default outside of maw / To fix: run `maw epoch sync`\", including both SHAs. Correct diagnosis and the exact remedy, no guessing. (This is the second sigil session in a row where the first merge needed it, because releases get committed straight to main — an auto-offer or `--sync-epoch` flag would remove a recurring step.)\n- **Uncommitted trunk files preserved across merges**, with a pinned recovery snapshot and an explicit NOTE naming the files. Two untracked bones files survived four merges untouched.\n- **`INVARIANT: verified N sibling workspace(s) — no committed work orphaned`** printed on every merge. For a lead agent running a merge train, that line is the single most reassuring thing maw prints.\n- **`maw release prepare` / `preflight` now work** against sigil's `[workspace]` + per-package `[package].version` layout with no manifest change (was maw bn-ace6, verified fixed in pre.12). `prepare`'s Cargo.lock regen is a precise one-line bump rather than a full re-resolve, which is exactly right here — a bare `cargo update` in sigil rewrites ~583 lock lines and breaks the sigil-browser build.\n\n# Suggested priority\n\nFinding 1 is the only one that can put broken code on main, and it does so while printing a success message. Findings 2 and 3 cost turns and erode trust in the pre-flight commands but are recoverable. Finding 4 is a papercut with a real edge for concurrent agents.\n\nFindings 2, 3 and 4 are each written with their own reproducer and impact so they can be split into separate bones per bn-25db AC#3 if that is preferred; they were kept together here because they were all encountered in one merge train and the interaction between them is part of the story.","kind":"bug","labels":["field-report"],"title":"[field-report] sigil merge train on pre.12: ws resolve --keep silently reverts hand-resolved content and reports ready to merge"}	blake3:xbOnl_C3yauLyipETZay2YzVlqNYHTWlsSNJM-AI4pU
1785605982385814	sigil-dev	itc:v3:AWLOeSZkkyTnmRAAAAAAQd3q2q1d7QAAAAExCQ		item.comment	bn-25db	{"body":"Dogfood friction report from sigil (maw 1.0.0-pre.12, 2026-07-31): bn-3v9e.\n\nFour findings from a 4-workspace merge train with three concurrent subagent workers. One is severe — 'maw ws resolve --keep <ws-name>' silently reverted a hand-resolved import line (it re-runs a 3-way merge rather than taking the named side verbatim) and then printed 'Workspace is ready for merge' for a tree that did not compile. It stayed off main only because the resolved file was re-read rather than trusted.\n\nPer AC#3 the other three (resolve writing a header its own merge gate rejects; ws conflicts disagreeing with merge, possibly a bn-8zqz regression; auto-sync committing conflict markers into a sibling's HEAD) each carry their own reproducer and measured impact inside bn-3v9e and can be split into separate bones if you prefer them tracked individually — they were kept together because the interaction between them is part of the failure story.\n\nAlso recorded what worked: 'maw ws overlap' predicted both collision pairs and exact paths before any merge and drove the merge ordering; the conflict-header guard's refusal to be bypassed by --force; and 'maw epoch sync's diagnostic. Those are load-bearing for multi-agent use."}	blake3:Q8_5Q6b2D4fp64omomyq3avV2n3CJ_DOUWiW8rb_DqE
